<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demaratus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 515–491 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demaratos, Damaratos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demaratus was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> from around 515 to 491 BCE, the 15th ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurypontid-dynasty">Eurypontid dynasty</a> and the firstborn son of King Ariston. He is best known for opposing his co-ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a>. When Demaratus hindered him from punishing Aegina for submitting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Cleomenes backed Leotychidas' claim that Demaratus was not Ariston's son and bribed the Delphic oracle, and Demaratus was deposed in 491 BCE. He fled to the Persian court, received the cities of Teuthrania and Halisarna near Pergamum, and went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE, where according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he warned the king not to underestimate the Spartans.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Demaratus was born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurypontid-dynasty">Eurypontid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> as the firstborn son of King Ariston. Herodotus reports that his birth was accompanied by a prophecy of future prominence, and that his name meant wished by the people. According to Herodotus, Ariston doubted that the child was his own. Remembering when he had married the woman and counting the months on his fingers, he swore an oath that Demaratus could not be his son. As the boy grew up Ariston repented the oath and treated him as his own. When Ariston died, Demaratus became the fifteenth ruler of the Eurypontid line, around 515 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Demaratus shared the kingship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a>, and the two became rivals. When Cleomenes tried to make Isagoras tyrant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Demaratus tried to frustrate the plan. Aegina was one of the states that gave earth and water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Athens appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> to punish this submission. Cleomenes crossed to the island to arrest those responsible, but Demaratus interfered and did his utmost to bring Cleomenes into disfavour at home.</p><p>In retaliation Cleomenes urged Leotychidas, a relative and personal enemy of Demaratus, to claim the throne on the ground that Demaratus was the son not of Ariston but of Agetus, his mother's first husband. Leotychidas had a private grievance: he had been promised Perkalon, the daughter of Chilon, but Demaratus had taken her as his own wife. Cleomenes bribed the Delphic oracle to pronounce in favour of Leotychidas, who became king in 491 BCE. Cleomenes then returned to Aegina with Leotychidas and seized ten leading citizens, whom he left at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as hostages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Forced to flee after his deposition, Demaratus went to the Persian court, where he was given the cities of Teuthrania and Halisarna, near Pergamum. He accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE. Herodotus records that Xerxes questioned him about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and that Demaratus spoke favourably of them even though they had exiled him. In Herodotus's account he told the king that the Spartans, though free, accepted law as their master, and that the law forbade them to flee in battle whatever the number of their enemies.</p><p>Demaratus is one of several Greek aristocrats who took refuge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> after reversals at home; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> and Gongylos were others. Such exiles were generally rewarded with land and rule over cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. No year is known for his death. His sons Eurysthenes and Prokles ruled Halisarna and Teuthrania after him, and his descendants held the area for about eighty years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demaratus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-503" aria-label="Sparta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Demaratus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-503">What was here in 503 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
